def register_poems(book):
    def P(title,author,year,era,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,a,url,quote,context,meaning,note=''):
        genre={'Lycidas':'Pastoral elegy','Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard':'Meditative elegy','Christabel':'Gothic narrative poem','To a Skylark':'Ode','Ode on a Grecian Urn':'Ode','Ulysses':'Dramatic monologue','My Last Duchess':'Dramatic monologue','Dover Beach':'Meditative lyric','The Windhover':'Sonnet'}.get(title,'Lyric poem')
        book(title,author,year,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,a,url,quote,context,meaning,note)
    P('Lycidas','John Milton',1638,'renaissance','An imagined pastoral landscape and the sea','A shepherd’s song becomes a struggle to answer a friend’s death.','The mourning speaker=Poet grieving a lost companion|Lycidas=Pastoral name for Edward King|The uncouth swain=Figure revealed in the closing frame', '''
The leaves are gathered too soon|Come into a pastoral world where mourning cannot wait! The speaker plucks poetic leaves before their season, making the beginning itself an image of premature loss.|The mourning speaker|forest
The companion will not return|Lycidas is dead, drowned at sea. A shepherd's name carries the memory of Edward King! The pastoral disguise offers a language for grief without undoing its reality.|Lycidas,The mourning speaker|sea
Shared mornings enter the song|The speaker remembers their imagined shepherd life together. Listen to the fellowship behind the sorrow! Loss hurts because work, learning and song had once been shared.|The mourning speaker,Lycidas|garden
Nature cannot answer everything|Flowers and landscapes mourn, yet the question persists: where were the protecting powers? The poem tests the comfort its own beautiful pastoral setting appears to offer.|The mourning speaker|storm
The poet asks about effort|Why labour for art if death can strike so soon? The friend's loss opens a question about vocation! Grief unsettles the value of the speaker's own future work.|The mourning speaker|book
Fame receives another measure|A divine voice directs ambition beyond earthly applause. The answer is religious rather than a guarantee of public success! What counts as lasting worth begins to change.|The mourning speaker|stars
The sea brings no simple rescue|Other voices examine the death without restoring the lost body. Keep the water in view! The poem moves through explanations while the fact of drowning remains irreparable.|Lycidas|sea
An angry witness enters|Saint Peter's speech condemns corrupt religious shepherds. The elegy suddenly confronts an institution! Mourning for one good life sharpens the anger toward careless spiritual leadership.|The mourning speaker|temple
Flowers make an imagined funeral|The speaker gathers a lush tribute. What tenderness the imagined ceremony offers! Yet uncertainty about the drowned body makes this burial symbolic rather than a recovered physical grave.|The mourning speaker,Lycidas|garden
The song refuses final extinction|The voice turns toward Christian resurrection, declaring Lycidas not finally lost. Hope arrives through faith! It is an answer to mortality, not a denial that the friend died.|The mourning speaker,Lycidas|sunrise
The mourner becomes visible|A closing narrator identifies an uncouth swain who has sung the lament. Step back and see the singer! The poem frames its own passage from grief toward renewed vocation.|The uncouth swain|book
A new morning opens|The swain rises toward fresh woods and pastures. Remember this movement! He carries the work of mourning into another day, rather than staying forever within its first cry.|The uncouth swain|road''','Why does the ending move toward another landscape?','The pastoral singer resumes life and vocation after a lament that passes through grief, criticism and religious hope.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/44733/lycidas','To-morrow to fresh woods, and pastures new.','Closing line.','The elegy ends by allowing the living poet to move forward.','Written 1637 for Edward King; first published 1638. Religious and pastoral figures represent poetic argument, not literal eyewitness events.')
    P('Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard','Thomas Gray',1751,'restoration','A rural churchyard at evening','The quiet graves ask whose greatness history never learned to see.','The speaker=Observer reflecting on neglected lives|The villagers=Rural people buried in the churchyard|The imagined passer-by=Possible witness to the speaker’s own life', '''
Evening empties the field|Come closer as the working day ends! Herds and labourers withdraw, leaving the speaker beside a quiet churchyard. The poem begins by making room to notice absence.|The speaker|sunset
The graves wait beneath the trees|The village dead lie beyond the reach of another morning's tasks. Who remembers them? Familiar rural sounds become reminders of the ordinary lives that will not resume.|The speaker,The villagers|grave
The household sounds fall silent|No hearth, returning children or busy domestic welcome can call the buried people back. Pause here! The poem measures death through the simple human attachments it interrupts.|The villagers|home
Their labour once shaped the land|The speaker recalls farming and useful work. These lives were not empty because they lacked fame! The cultivated fields themselves hold evidence of effort and skill.|The villagers|garden
Ambition is told to listen|The poem challenges those who mock humble lives. What makes a public title worth more than affection or labour? The churchyard lowers the distance between high and low status.|The speaker|crown
The grand also reach the grave|Wealth, beauty and power cannot avoid death. The warning reaches beyond the village! Monumental remembrance does not grant the buried person another chance to live.|The speaker|stone
Hidden gifts are imagined|Perhaps remarkable abilities lay here without opportunity! Poverty can prevent talent from growing or being recognised, making silence an effect of social circumstance rather than proof of insignificance.|The villagers|book
A great name might have stayed unknown|The speaker imagines unrecognised counterparts of famous public figures. What could access have changed? The thought widens the churchyard into a question about opportunity and historical memory.|The speaker,The villagers|letter
Obscurity has another side|Restricted lives may also have avoided the crimes of powerful ambition. The judgement grows more complicated! Public greatness brings possibilities for destructive action as well as achievement.|The speaker|sword
Small memorials still speak|Simple inscriptions ask for remembrance. Look at that modest wish! The need to be loved and recalled does not belong only to people whose monuments dominate the skyline.|The villagers|stone
The observer becomes a remembered person|An imagined passer-by tells of the speaker himself. The poem turns its gaze back! Someone who watched forgotten lives will also depend upon another person's account.|The speaker,The imagined passer-by|road
The epitaph asks for tenderness|The closing epitaph offers a modest, compassionate portrait. Carry that tenderness away! The final memorial makes sympathy more valuable than pretending to know every life completely.|The speaker|grave''','What does the poem ask us to reconsider about fame?','A lack of fame may reflect restricted opportunity, not a lack of talent or human value.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/44299/elegy-written-in-a-country-churchyard','The paths of glory lead but to the grave.','Early stanza addressing pride and public ambition.','Death joins the famous and forgotten, making human value larger than public recognition.')
    P('I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud','William Wordsworth',1807,'romantic','A lakeside walk and its later recollection','A golden crowd keeps dancing long after the walk has ended!','The speaker=Walker who later remembers|The daffodils=Flowers becoming a stored image of joy', '''
The walker feels apart|Imagine drifting like a cloud above the landscape! The speaker begins in solitude, moving without the companionship that will soon arrive from an unexpected direction.|The speaker|mountain
Gold appears beside the water|Suddenly—a host of daffodils! The encounter interrupts loneliness with colour and movement, turning an ordinary walk into something the mind cannot help noticing.|The speaker,The daffodils|garden
The breeze gives them motion|The flowers flutter and dance in the breeze. Watch that liveliness! The description treats the daffodils as animated companions without making them literal talking people.|The daffodils|wind
The crowd seems unending|The line of flowers stretches along the bay like stars. How far can delight reach? Their abundance enlarges the small discovery into an image of unexpected generosity.|The daffodils|stars
The lake joins the dance|Waves move beside the flowers, but the daffodils seem brighter in spirit. The whole scene comes alive! Nature offers a fellowship the solitary walker had not been seeking.|The speaker,The daffodils|river
The mood quietly changes|A poet cannot remain untouched by such company. Solitude is no longer simply isolation! The encounter gives the speaker a temporary participation in the movement around him.|The speaker|heart
The eyes remain on the flowers|He gazes and gazes. What is being gathered without a conscious plan? The scene enters memory before the speaker understands how useful it may later become.|The speaker,The daffodils|mirror
The gift is not yet understood|At the lake he does not fully recognise the encounter's wealth. Here is the lovely delay! An experience can matter more later than it seemed to matter at the time.|The speaker|coins
Another day brings stillness|Later he lies on a couch, vacant or thoughtful. The walk is over! No flowers physically enter the room, yet an inward image can change the quiet present.|The speaker|bed
The inward eye opens|The daffodils flash into memory. There they are again! The remembered scene brings a form of companionship into solitude without requiring the original landscape to return.|The speaker,The daffodils|garden
The heart takes up the movement|Joy fills the speaker and his heart dances with the flowers. Notice the transfer! The body's earlier looking becomes an inward rhythm of remembered delight.|The speaker,The daffodils|heart
Carry the golden crowd with you|The poem closes inside memory. What image from your own life returns like that? Its story is an encounter, a delay and a renewed joy—not merely a pretty field.|The speaker,The daffodils|sunrise''','Why is the final indoor scene essential?','It shows the original encounter continuing to restore joy through memory.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45521/i-wandered-lonely-as-a-cloud','They flash upon that inward eye','Final stanza.','Remembered beauty becomes a continuing resource in solitude.','First published 1807; familiar revised version appeared in 1815. Twelve frames follow images and recollection, not additional invented incidents.')
    P('Christabel','S. T. Coleridge',1816,'romantic','A castle and a forest at night','A stranger asks for shelter—but what has crossed the threshold?','Christabel=Young woman who offers refuge|Geraldine=Mysterious guest|Sir Leoline=Christabel’s father|Bracy=Dreaming bard who offers a warning', '''
The castle sleeps uneasily|The night is strange, and Christabel goes into the woods to pray. Listen to the stillness! A private errand takes her beyond the household's protection.|Christabel|forest
A voice comes from the shadows|She discovers Geraldine, who tells of an abduction. Christabel responds with pity! But a stranger's story is also the only explanation she has for the danger before her.|Christabel,Geraldine|ghost
Compassion opens the gate|Christabel brings Geraldine toward the castle. Shelter seems the generous answer! Yet a troubling difficulty at the threshold makes the act of welcome feel less straightforward.|Christabel,Geraldine|gate
The sleeping house admits its guest|They pass into the quiet household. The unfamiliar figure is now inside! Hospitality has moved danger from the remote forest into Christabel's intimate domestic world.|Christabel,Geraldine|home
The chamber holds a secret|In the bedchamber Geraldine reveals something the narrator refuses to describe fully. What has Christabel seen? The withheld detail gives uncertainty more power than a clear explanation would.|Christabel,Geraldine|bed
Speech becomes constrained|Geraldine's influence binds Christabel's ability to tell what happened. Fear is joined by silence! Knowing danger is not enough if the witness cannot make others understand.|Christabel,Geraldine|chains
Morning does not clear the darkness|Geraldine meets Sir Leoline, who accepts her claimed family connection. The guest wins authority! Christabel's distress has to compete with the stranger's persuasive account.|Geraldine,Sir Leoline|hall
Old friendship strengthens the claim|Leoline remembers Geraldine's supposed father and a broken friendship. Nostalgia now supports his trust! The present stranger benefits from an emotionally charged story about the past.|Geraldine,Sir Leoline|letter
Bracy brings a disturbing dream|The bard describes a dove endangered by a snake. A warning arrives through symbols! Will the household hear danger, or bend the image toward what it already wishes to believe?|Bracy,Sir Leoline|bird
The daughter cannot persuade her father|Christabel's resistance angers Leoline. How lonely her position becomes! A father's confidence in the guest turns his daughter's fear into something he treats as disobedience.|Christabel,Sir Leoline,Geraldine|storm
The uncanny appears in imitation|Christabel mirrors Geraldine's serpent-like look. Identity itself feels unsettled! The poem leaves us watching an influence whose nature and reach cannot be securely explained.|Christabel,Geraldine|mirror
The door remains unanswered|The surviving poem ends without resolving Geraldine's identity or Christabel's danger. Do not invent a rescue! Remember the unfinished struggle between hospitality, authority and a silenced witness.|Christabel,Geraldine|gate''','Why must the storyboard leave the ending unresolved?','Coleridge’s surviving poem is unfinished, so neither Geraldine’s nature nor the ultimate outcome is settled.',11101,'A sight to dream of, not to tell!','Part I, the withheld description in the chamber.','The refusal to describe creates dread and connects seeing with the difficulty of speaking.','Written in parts c. 1797 and 1800; first published 1816. The poem remains unfinished.')
    P('To a Skylark','Percy Bysshe Shelley',1820,'romantic','An evening sky and the speaker’s imagination','A bird disappears into the sky, but its song refuses to leave!','The speaker=Listener seeking the secret of joy|The skylark=Unseen source of extraordinary song', '''
The greeting reaches upward|Look up! The speaker greets the skylark as if its song belongs to something beyond an ordinary bird. Wonder begins by questioning what the ear has encountered.|The speaker,The skylark|bird
The singer climbs the sky|The skylark rises while pouring out music. Its motion and sound seem inseparable! The song offers an image of energy that does not pause to calculate its reward.|The skylark|sunrise
The light hides the body|The bird moves beyond clear sight, though its voice remains. Where is that singer? Absence becomes part of the enchantment, letting sound exceed the visible creature producing it.|The speaker,The skylark|stars
The listener searches for likenesses|The speaker asks what can resemble such music. One comparison will not do! The imagination begins moving through other hidden sources of beauty and feeling.|The speaker|book
A poet sings unseen|The bird is compared to a poet hidden in thought. What can a voice change without showing its maker? The comparison joins natural song with the social reach of art.|The speaker|letter
A private song fills a chamber|Another image imagines a secluded young woman singing. The heard beauty comes from an unseen life! Sound can cross the limits that keep its source withdrawn.|The speaker|home
Small hidden beauties answer|A glow-worm and a sheltered rose offer further likenesses. Keep collecting the images! Each gives a new way to understand beauty reaching outward from concealment.|The skylark|garden
The song outruns the comparisons|Wedding and victory songs seem insufficient beside the skylark. Its delight remains beyond the listener's examples! The growing list shows both imaginative richness and the failure to capture the sound.|The speaker,The skylark|heart
The human listener remembers sorrow|People mix pleasure with pain and longing. How different the bird seems! The poem's praise reveals the listener's own emotional limits as much as the singer's apparent freedom.|The speaker|storm
Joy becomes a question|What could teach such untroubled gladness? The speaker asks the bird for understanding! Its happiness is an imagined ideal, not scientific proof that birds never experience distress.|The speaker,The skylark|mirror
Half the happiness would be enough|The poet asks to learn even part of that joy. What a wish! A transformed song might make the world listen as intensely as he listens now.|The speaker|bird
The request remains in the air|The poem ends with the desired lesson still a request. Carry the upward motion with you! Aspiration, comparison and human sorrow keep the skylark's imagined music beyond possession.|The speaker,The skylark|wind''','Why does the speaker ask to learn from the bird?','Its imagined untroubled joy contrasts with human pleasure mixed with sorrow.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45146/to-a-skylark','Our sweetest songs are those that tell of saddest thought.','Late stanza contrasting human song and the skylark.','Human art finds beauty within the sorrow the speaker hopes to transcend.')
    P('Ode on a Grecian Urn','John Keats',1820,'romantic','Painted scenes on an ancient urn','A silent object tells stories that can never quite finish!','The speaker=Questioner studying the urn|The lovers=Figures whose pursuit remains unfinished|The musician=Player held in the image|The procession=Painted people approaching sacrifice', '''
The silent storyteller waits|Step closer to the urn! Its figures survive from an earlier world, but it cannot answer in ordinary speech. The speaker begins by questioning its imagined histories.|The speaker|urn
The picture raises more questions|Who are these people, and what pursuit fills the image? The scene offers action without explanation! Our wish for a complete story encounters art's permanent silence.|The speaker,The lovers|mirror
The music cannot be heard|A painted musician keeps playing for the imagination. What sound does silence permit? The unheard tune cannot disappoint the listener in quite the way an actual performance might.|The musician|music
The kiss cannot arrive|The lover approaches but never reaches his beloved. What a strange bargain! Desire avoids the fading that follows fulfilment, but only because fulfilment itself is denied.|The lovers|heart
The trees cannot lose their leaves|The carved branches remain green. The season never changes! Preservation looks enviable until we notice that the pictured life also cannot develop beyond its fixed moment.|The speaker|garden
The song cannot tire|The musician's tune seems forever fresh. Imagine endless beginning! Yet this freshness belongs to an image beyond living effort, not to a human body exempt from fatigue.|The musician|music
Human passion has a different cost|The speaker contrasts the ideal scene with desire's exhaustion. The urn's world looks safe from that ache! Its permanence gains meaning through the vulnerability of actual experience.|The speaker,The lovers|fire
A procession moves without moving|The gaze shifts to people heading toward sacrifice. Another story begins! The solemn destination remains imagined because the pictured procession can never reach its next event.|The procession|temple
A town is imagined elsewhere|If these people left a town, who remains there? The speaker invents a question rather than recovering a fact! The absent place reveals how pictures provoke stories beyond their boundaries.|The speaker|village
The empty street cannot explain itself|No pictured citizen returns to tell why the town is quiet. The imagined vacancy stays unresolved! Art preserves a scene while withholding the ordinary sequence that would complete it.|The speaker|road
The object will outlive this viewer|The speaker imagines the urn enduring later generations and new sorrows. Its survival is impressive! But lasting material form does not remove the suffering of the people who encounter it.|The speaker|stone
The final words keep us debating|Beauty and truth meet in the ending's famous assertion. Who speaks, and how far does the claim reach? Keep the question alive instead of turning a difficult poem into a simple slogan!|The speaker|book''','What does permanence cost the figures on the urn?','They avoid ageing and change but cannot complete the actions or desires pictured.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/44477/ode-on-a-grecian-urn','Heard melodies are sweet, but those unheard','Second stanza; the sentence continues by preferring unheard melodies.','The silent image stimulates an ideal music that exists within imagination.','Written 1819; first published 1820. The final statement’s speaker and meaning are debated.')
    P('She Walks in Beauty','Lord Byron',1815,'romantic','An imagined portrait of a woman','Starlight and darkness meet in a single luminous portrait!','The speaker=Observer creating an idealised portrait|The woman=Subject of the poem whose own voice is absent', '''
Night becomes a compliment|Imagine a clear, starry night! The speaker begins the portrait through darkness lit by brightness, making beauty a harmony rather than merely a dazzling surface.|The speaker,The woman|stars
Two opposites meet|Light and dark seem to unite in the woman's appearance. Notice the balance! The poem builds its ideal from qualities usually treated as opposing conditions.|The woman|moon
The gaze settles on the eyes|The harmony gathers in her look. What can an observer truly know from eyes? The image moves toward an inward life while remaining an external act of seeing.|The speaker,The woman|mirror
Daylight seems too bright|The portrait favours a tender illumination over gaudy daylight. The preference matters! Its beauty depends upon restraint and proportion, not the largest possible quantity of brilliance.|The woman|sunset
One change might disturb the whole|The speaker imagines too much shade or too little light spoiling the effect. Such precision! The portrait's harmony is presented as delicate rather than unlimited.|The speaker|book
The dark hair enters the picture|The balance rests in raven hair and the softly lit face. Follow the details! Physical description gives the abstract harmony something the reader can actually imagine.|The woman|cloth
The face becomes a claimed window|The speaker imagines thoughts expressing themselves serenely through appearance. But this is an interpretation! The woman has not spoken, so the inward portrait belongs to the observer's judgement.|The speaker,The woman|mirror
The cheek holds a quiet expression|Softness and calm gather in the facial image. There is no dramatic action to invent! The poem asks us to attend to an idealised presence through small changes of focus.|The woman|heart
The smiles suggest kindness|The speaker reads goodness in smiles and colour. How readily beauty becomes moral evidence! The portrait joins outward charm with a virtue that the observer assumes rather than independently proves.|The speaker,The woman|garden
The calm becomes a whole life|The imagined goodness widens into peaceful days and innocent affection. The poem's certainty grows! Its ending tells us how the speaker wants appearance and character to agree.|The woman|home
The missing voice deserves attention|Pause and notice who never speaks! The woman's inward life reaches us through someone else's idealising description, a limitation that belongs to the poem's form.|The speaker,The woman|book
Keep the balanced night in memory|There is no marriage plot or secret disaster here! Remember an image shaped by light, darkness and the speaker's wish to see outward beauty as inward harmony.|The speaker,The woman|stars''','Why distinguish the speaker’s judgement from the woman’s own account?','The poem gives an idealised outward portrait; the woman does not speak for herself.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/43844/she-walks-in-beauty','She walks in beauty, like the night','Opening line.','The central comparison makes beauty a balance of darkness and light.','Written 1814; published in Hebrew Melodies in 1815. The frames explore description rather than inventing events.')
    P('Ulysses','Alfred, Lord Tennyson',1842,'victorian','Ithaca and an imagined final voyage','The old voyager looks at home—and hears the sea calling again!','Ulysses=Ageing hero longing for another voyage|Telemachus=Son suited to governing Ithaca|The mariners=Companions addressed by Ulysses', '''
Home feels too narrow|Meet a returned king who cannot rest! Ulysses describes governing Ithaca with impatience, making homecoming the beginning of another dissatisfaction rather than the end of desire.|Ulysses|home
The ordinary duties trouble him|He finds little delight in distributing laws among his people. Listen critically! His frustration reveals a powerful temperament, but it also raises questions about obligations to those he rules.|Ulysses|crown
The traveller remembers appetite|Past joys and hardships return together. He wants to experience life deeply! The remembered voyage becomes evidence for a self that cannot be satisfied with mere survival.|Ulysses|sea
The encounters have shaped him|Ulysses claims that what he met has entered his identity. What a wide self-image! Experience matters because it changes the traveller rather than simply decorating his history.|Ulysses|road
The horizon keeps withdrawing|Knowledge opens another untravelled world. The desire has no natural finish! Each discovery makes the remaining unknown feel larger, leaving completion always beyond the current view.|Ulysses|stars
Age makes the wish urgent|Life remaining is limited, yet Ulysses refuses inactivity. The stakes sharpen! His eloquence turns approaching death into a reason for movement, while the risks of that movement remain real.|Ulysses|clock
The son receives the kingdom|Telemachus is introduced as capable of patient governance. Ulysses values a different kind of work! The distinction allows another voyage, but does not erase the tension between adventure and duty.|Ulysses,Telemachus|crown
The harbour gathers the companions|The address turns toward his mariners. Imagine the waiting ships! A private dissatisfaction becomes a speech that asks other people to share the undertaking and its danger.|Ulysses,The mariners|ship
The old courage is summoned|He remembers their shared strength and labour. Their bodies have changed! The speech seeks a continuing identity in courage when physical power can no longer guarantee success.|Ulysses,The mariners|sword
The last voyage has two possibilities|They may reach another shore—or be overwhelmed. The poem leaves both open! Desire for discovery cannot be treated as proof that the proposed journey will end safely.|Ulysses,The mariners|storm
The will rises above weakness|The closing declaration gathers resolve from diminished strength. Hear its momentum! The ageing hero makes perseverance sound irresistible, though listeners still have to judge what the perseverance serves.|Ulysses|sunset
The ship stays within the speech|We do not witness the voyage's outcome. Remember the form! This is Ulysses persuading companions, an inspiring and unsettling appeal whose confidence is itself something to examine.|Ulysses,The mariners|ship''','Why should the closing speech be examined as well as admired?','It expresses courage but also persuades others toward an uncertain voyage and away from settled duties.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45392/ulysses','To strive, to seek, to find, and not to yield.','Final line.','Resolve gives the speech its force; the poem leaves the undertaking’s ethical questions open.','Written 1833; published 1842. A dramatic monologue, not a literal sequel to Homer.')
    P('My Last Duchess','Robert Browning',1842,'victorian','A Renaissance Italian duke’s art collection','He shows a portrait—but accidentally reveals himself!','The Duke=Aristocrat controlling the conversation|The Duchess=Dead wife represented in a portrait|The envoy=Listener negotiating another marriage', '''
The curtain opens on a face|The Duke shows an envoy his late wife's portrait. Come closer—but notice who controls the curtain! The painting enters the story through its owner's command over looking.|The Duke,The Duchess,The envoy|portrait
The painted presence looks alive|He praises the artist's skill. The Duchess appears vivid! Yet the living woman is absent, and the apparent animation belongs to a possession the Duke can display at will.|The Duchess,The Duke|portrait
The visitor receives an explanation|The Duke interprets her smile before the envoy can ask freely. Listen to that control! He manages both the object and the story that someone else is allowed to hear.|The Duke,The envoy|book
Small pleasures become accusations|The Duchess enjoyed ordinary gifts and attention. Why should kindness anger her husband? His account turns delight into evidence because it does not revolve exclusively around him.|The Duchess,The Duke|garden
The old family name demands priority|The Duke thinks his ancient rank deserved unique appreciation. His grievance grows clearer! He wants hierarchy, rather than shared joy, to govern the meaning of her affection.|The Duke|crown
Conversation is treated as humiliation|He refuses to explain his wishes, calling such discussion a lowering of himself. What a revealing choice! Pride prevents the ordinary negotiation that could make a relationship mutual.|The Duke|chains
The smiles are abruptly stopped|He says he gave commands and the smiles stopped. The account turns chilling! It strongly suggests lethal coercion without providing an explicit, independently witnessed description of the Duchess's death.|The Duke,The Duchess|grave
The portrait is easier to govern|Now the Duchess smiles only when he permits the curtain to open. The contrast is terrible! Art gives him a controlled image where a living person had her own responses.|The Duke,The Duchess|portrait
The next marriage enters the speech|The envoy represents a potential new bride's family. The Duke's history becomes a warning! What future is being negotiated by a man who treats an earlier wife's autonomy as an offence?|The Duke,The envoy|contract
The dowry is delicately mentioned|He insists the daughter herself is his object while discussing wealth. Hear the polished language! Possession and social transaction remain near the centre of the courtly exchange.|The Duke,The envoy|coins
Another artwork completes the pattern|He points to Neptune taming a sea-horse. Keep that image! The admired act of domination echoes the account of marriage we have just heard.|The Duke|stone
The storyteller has become the evidence|The envoy's response stays largely silent, but we can judge the speaker's words. Remember the trick! A confident self-defence has revealed pride, control and the threat beneath refinement.|The Duke,The envoy|mirror''','How does the Duke reveal what he means to conceal?','His complaints and controlling language expose possessiveness and coercion while he thinks he is displaying refinement.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/43768/my-last-duchess','I gave commands;','The Duke’s account of the Duchess’s smiles.','The terse statement exposes the violence behind his polished manner.','The poem strongly implies the Duke caused her death; the precise act is not narrated. Historical associations do not make the speech a factual transcript.')
    P('Dover Beach','Matthew Arnold',1867,'victorian','The shore at Dover at night','The sea looks calm—but listen to what the waves are taking away.','The speaker=Observer moving from beauty to uncertainty|The beloved=Person addressed as a hoped-for source of fidelity', '''
The window opens toward the sea|Imagine a calm night above the Channel! Moonlight and a distant coast create apparent peace, inviting the listener into a scene whose emotional ground will soon shift.|The speaker,The beloved|moon
A light crosses the water|The French coast briefly glimmers while England's cliffs stand nearby. The scene holds both beauty and distance! What looks settled is already flickering at its edges.|The speaker|sea
The invitation asks us to listen|The speaker calls the beloved closer. Now use your ears! The visual calm cannot contain the meaning that the movement of the water will bring.|The speaker,The beloved|home
The pebbles make a harsher music|Waves draw stones back and throw them forward. Listen to the grating rhythm! Repeated motion unsettles the lovely surface instead of simply adding another pleasant detail.|The speaker|stone
The sound carries sadness|The rhythm brings an old note of sorrow. Beauty and grief now occupy the same shore! The speaker's response turns a natural sound into an emotional revelation.|The speaker|heart
Another listener enters memory|Sophocles is imagined hearing sorrow in the Aegean. The present expands across time! A private night at Dover becomes part of a longer human encounter with suffering.|The speaker|book
Faith is imagined as another sea|The poem recalls belief once surrounding the world like a protective shoreline. What a powerful image! Spiritual confidence takes a shape we can hear withdrawing.|The speaker|river
The tide of certainty retreats|The Sea of Faith seems to recede. The speaker hears its leaving! This is a historical and spiritual interpretation, not evidence that everyone in the period held exactly one belief.|The speaker|wind
The exposed shore feels vulnerable|As the protecting sea withdraws, the world seems bare. The earlier moonlit calm changes meaning! The same beautiful setting now holds a perception of abandonment and insecurity.|The speaker|grave
The beloved becomes an appeal|The speaker asks for mutual truthfulness. Come back to the two people at the window! Their fidelity is offered as a fragile response to a larger uncertainty they cannot solve.|The speaker,The beloved|heart
The beautiful world cannot promise safety|Apparent splendour is said to lack secure joy, peace and help. The judgement is bleak! Its force belongs to this speaker's crisis rather than an unquestionable description of every life.|The speaker|mirror
The darkness becomes a battlefield|The final image imagines confused armies clashing at night. What happened to the calm sea? Remember the movement from lovely surface through troubling sound to an urgent appeal for fidelity.|The speaker,The beloved|storm''','Why does the poem move from seeing to hearing?','The disturbing wave sound breaks the initial appearance of calm and opens reflections on grief and lost certainty.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/43588/dover-beach','Ah, love, let us be true','The final stanza’s appeal.','Mutual fidelity becomes a fragile human response to spiritual and historical uncertainty.','Published 1867; probably composed earlier, often dated c. 1851. The speaker’s judgement is not a universal historical fact.')
    P('The Windhover','Gerard Manley Hopkins',1918,'victorian','A morning sky and an inward act of praise','A hovering falcon makes the morning blaze with wonder!','The speaker=Watcher whose admiration becomes spiritual|The kestrel=Bird hovering and turning in the wind', '''
Morning catches the watcher|Look into the morning sky! The speaker catches sight of a kestrel, and a moment of ordinary observation begins to demand language more energetic than a calm description.|The speaker,The kestrel|sunrise
The bird rides the air|The falcon holds itself against the wind. What astonishing control! The poem's movement follows a creature whose apparent mastery depends upon continually responding to an invisible force.|The kestrel|bird
The motion resembles riding|Images of a rider and rein give flight a bodily analogy. Follow the balancing act! The bird's skill becomes something the reader can feel through another familiar form of movement.|The kestrel|wind
The turn changes the rhythm|The kestrel sweeps and pivots through the air. Watch the sudden change! The verse presses motion into its own sound, making observation feel like an excited physical participation.|The kestrel|bird
The watcher’s heart answers|The speaker's heart responds to the bird's achievement. The scene is now inward as well as outward! Attention to another being reveals a feeling that ordinary life had held back.|The speaker|heart
Many qualities gather at once|Beauty, action, pride, air and strength meet in the description. The praise accelerates! The poem tries to hold a whole living intensity rather than selecting one neatly isolated quality.|The kestrel|stars
The word buckle turns the poem|A dense, ambiguous turning word gathers the praise. Do the qualities unite, yield or break open? The uncertainty is part of the sonnet's force, not a puzzle solved by one paraphrase.|The speaker|book
The brightness exceeds the first sight|The speaker imagines a greater, more dangerous beauty. Wonder becomes spiritually charged! The dedication to Christ helps place the bird's splendour within a larger act of devotion.|The speaker,The kestrel|fire
The plough brings us back to earth|The closing movement shifts to ordinary labour. What a change of scale! A worked surface can shine, offering a new image of beauty disclosed through effort.|The speaker|garden
The ember also reveals a hidden light|A dull ember breaks and shows luminous colour. Look again at the apparently unremarkable! The final images suggest that brilliance can emerge through pressure and change.|The speaker|fire
The bird and the ground belong together|Flight, plough and ember have become linked acts of attention. The poem's wonder is not confined to a distant sky! Ordinary material life can disclose intensity too.|The speaker,The kestrel|stone
Let the rhythm carry the memory|Remember the hover, the turn and the sudden glow! The sonnet transforms watching into praise through compressed sound and imagery; it does not narrate a separate adventure for the bird.|The speaker,The kestrel|bird''','Why do the final plough and ember images matter?','They extend the bird’s revealed splendour into ordinary matter, effort and transformation.','https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/44402/the-windhover','My heart in hiding','The speaker’s response to the kestrel.','Watching the bird releases an inward admiration that had been concealed.','Composed 1877; published posthumously in 1918. Classified with Victorian poetry by composition and author, despite the later publication date. The meaning of buckle is debated.')
